{% block prose %}
<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>The honest version:</strong> if you're fully booked and not growing, no. If you rely on referrals, a website isn't there to replace them — it's there to stop them leaking at the moment someone checks you out. That's a narrower claim than most agencies make, and it's the one that's actually true.</p>
</div>

<h2>The word-of-mouth argument, taken seriously</h2>
<p>"Most of my work comes through word of mouth" is usually true, and it's a genuinely good position. Referred customers arrive pre-sold, haggle less, and are more pleasant to work for. Any tradie telling you word of mouth doesn't matter is selling something.</p>
<p>The problem isn't that word of mouth stops working. It's what happens in the gap between the recommendation and the phone call.</p>

<h2>What actually happens after someone recommends you</h2>
<p>Your customer tells their neighbour about you. The neighbour doesn't ring straight away — they type your name into their phone that evening.</p>
<p>What comes up decides whether you get the call:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Nothing at all.</strong> Not damning, but it's a small doubt at exactly the wrong moment.</li>
  <li><strong>A Facebook page last posted to in 2023.</strong> Worse than nothing. It reads as a business that might not be operating.</li>
  <li><strong>A Google Business Profile with two reviews and no photos.</strong> Fine, but nothing to build confidence on.</li>
  <li><strong>A real website with your work, your area and a way to contact you.</strong> The recommendation is now confirmed rather than merely received.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nearly three-quarters of New Zealanders say a business's website is the most important way they engage with it (<a href="/nz-tradie-website-statistics/">InternetNZ, 2025</a>). That's the checking step, and it happens whether you have a website or not.</p>

<h2>The three ways word of mouth dries up</h2>
<p>Referral work feels stable right up until it isn't, and it usually fails for one of three reasons:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Your network ages with you.</strong> The builders who feed you work retire. The property manager who used you for a decade moves on.</li>
  <li><strong>You want to change what you do.</strong> More renovations, fewer callouts. Bigger jobs, better margins. Your existing network refers you for what they already know you for.</li>
  <li><strong>Something changes suddenly.</strong> A main client goes under, a region slows, an injury takes you off the tools for two months. Referrals are the slowest lead source to restart from cold.</li>
</ol>
<p>A website doesn't prevent any of that. It means you have a second channel when it happens, rather than starting from zero.</p>

<h2>When a tradie genuinely doesn't need one</h2>
<p>We build these for a living and we'll still say it plainly — don't bother if:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You're booked solid for the next twelve months and happy.</strong> More enquiries would just be more people to turn down.</li>
  <li><strong>You're winding down.</strong> Within a couple of years of retiring, this won't pay back.</li>
  <li><strong>You're a subcontractor to two or three builders.</strong> If your work comes through commercial relationships, your effort belongs in those relationships.</li>
  <li><strong>You haven't got a Google Business Profile yet.</strong> Set that up first. It's free, it's faster, and for some trades it's genuinely the bigger lever.</li>
</ul>
<p>That last one matters. If you're choosing between a website and a properly managed Google Business Profile, and you can only do one, do the profile. We'd rather tell you that than take the money.</p>

<h2>Isn't a Facebook page enough?</h2>
<p>No, and the gap widens every year. Covered properly in <a href="/guides/facebook-page-vs-website-for-tradies/">Facebook page vs website for tradies</a>, but briefly: you don't own it, you can't rank on Google with it, it shows competitors' ads beside your work, and if the account is disabled you lose the lot with no appeal.</p>

<h2>What a website realistically does for a tradie</h2>
<p>Being straight about the ceiling as well as the floor:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>It will</th><th>It won't</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Confirm you're real when someone checks you out</td><td>Make the phone ring on day one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Get you found for "[trade] [suburb]" searches over time</td><td>Beat established competitors immediately</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Let you show work photos that win jobs before you quote</td><td>Win jobs you'd have lost on price</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Give you somewhere to send people from a Google profile</td><td>Replace asking customers for reviews</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keep working while you're under a house</td><td>Do anything if nobody maintains it</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>Anyone promising a flood of work in week one is guessing. A new site takes months to build ranking — the honest timeline is three to six months for meaningful organic movement, faster if you already have reviews and a Google profile with history.</p>

<h2>The actual test</h2>
<p>Forget the marketing. Ask yourself one question: <strong>if you needed three more jobs next month, where would they come from?</strong></p>
<p>If you have a confident answer, you don't need this yet. If the answer is "I'd ring around and hope", that's the gap — and it's the gap that gets expensive at exactly the moment you can least afford it.</p>

